Q: How does DI / beans work in Quarkus (vs Spring)?

Quarkus uses **CDI** (Jakarta Contexts and Dependency Injection), implemented by **ArC** (build-time CDI).
Similar goal to Spring IoC: create beans and inject dependencies — different APIs and lifecycle processing time.

────────────────────────────────────────
Basic bean

@ApplicationScoped   // ≈ Spring singleton service
public class OrderService {

    @Inject
    InventoryService inventoryService;

    public void place() { ... }
}

Injection:
@Inject OrderService orderService;   // field
// or constructor injection (preferred style)

────────────────────────────────────────
Common scopes (know these)

| CDI scope            | Rough Spring analogy     |
| -------------------- | ------------------------ |
| `@ApplicationScoped` | singleton bean           |
| `@Singleton`         | eager-ish singleton nuance (CDI `@Singleton` ≠ Spring exactly; often prefer ApplicationScoped) |
| `@RequestScoped`     | request scope            |
| `@Dependent`         | new instance / dependent |

Interview-safe default for services: **`@ApplicationScoped`**

────────────────────────────────────────
Spring contrast

| Spring                | Quarkus CDI              |
| --------------------- | ------------------------ |
| Component scan + stereotypes | Beans discovered at build |
| Runtime proxies common | Build-time generated wiring |
| `@Autowired`          | `@Inject`                |
| Self-invocation proxy pitfalls | Also be careful with interceptors/self calls |

────────────────────────────────────────
Producers (≈ @Bean methods)

public class ConfigProducer {
    @Produces
    @ApplicationScoped
    public Clock clock() {
        return Clock.systemUTC();
    }
}

────────────────────────────────────────
One-liner

“Quarkus DI is CDI with @Inject and scopes like @ApplicationScoped; wiring is largely decided at build time, unlike Spring’s runtime ApplicationContext refresh.”
